<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day20" xml:id="day-20-lecture">
  <title>Day 20</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      7.1: Polynomial regression
    </p>
    <p>
        7.2: Step functions
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <title>Covered in this class</title>

  <ul>
    <li>Why do people want to use polynomial regression rather than simple linear regression?</li>
    <li>How do you fit polynomial regression models?</li>
    <li>Why do people want to use step functions rather than simple linear regression?</li>
    <li>How do you fit step function models?</li>
    <li>What do polynomial regression and step functions have in common?</li>
    <li>What are the differences between polynomial regression and step functions?</li>
  </ul>

  </objectives>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Polynomial regression</title>

        <p>Replace the linear model</p>
        <p>
          <md>
            y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \varepsilon_i
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>with</p>
        <p>
          <md>
            y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i + \beta_2 x_i^2 + \cdots + \beta_d x_i^d + \varepsilon_i
          </md>
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Can learn with linear models by passing in predictors <m>x_i^\ell</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Tend not to go higher than degree 3 or 4 because this can make the model overly flexible.</p></li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Resulting model is global.
                        <ul>
                            <li>
                                <p>
                                    Can miss important localized information.
                                </p>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </li>
          </ul>
        </p>

  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Example: Wage Data</title>
    <p>
      <md>
        \texttt{wage} = \beta_0 + \beta_1\texttt{age} + \beta_2\texttt{age}^2 + \cdots + \beta_p\texttt{age}^p + \varepsilon.
      </md>
    </p>
        <p>
            Using OLS to fit a polynomial model: Treat higher powers as separate predictors. 
          <ul>
            <li><p>Make a matrix of predictors <m>X</m> with columns <m>X^0, X^1, X^2, \ldots, X^p</m>.</p>
                <tabular>
                    <row><cell></cell><cell><m>X_0</m></cell><cell><m>X_1</m></cell><cell><m>X_1^2</m></cell><cell><m>\ldots</m></cell><cell><m>X_1^p</m></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>1</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>2</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>\vdots</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                        <row><cell><m>n</m></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row>
                </tabular>
                
                </li>
            <li><p>Then use linear regression to learn the coefficients.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
    <p>The code learned:
      <md>
        <mrow>\widehat{\texttt{wage}} \amp= -184.1542 + 21.24552\,\texttt{age}</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp- 0.56386\,\texttt{age}^2 + 0.00681\,\texttt{age}^3</mrow>
        <mrow>\amp- 3\times 10^{-5}\,\texttt{age}^4</mrow>        
      </md>
    </p>    

              <image source="figures/Fig7-1a.png" width="60%">
            <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Wage versus Age showing a non-linear polynomial fit with dashed standard error bands.</shortdescription>
            <description><p>Scatter plot of the <c>Wage</c> data depicting wage versus age for men in the Mid-Atlantic region of the United States. The solid blue curve is a degree-4 polynomial of wage (in thousands of dollars) as a function of age, fit by least squares. The dashed curves indicate an estimated 95% confidence interval</p></description>
          </image>      

          <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>The dark line is a degree 4 polynomial.</p></li>
            <li><p>There is an estimated variance for each coefficient.
            <ul>
            <li><p>Assume the fitted model is
              <md>
                \hat f(x_0) = \hat\beta_0 + \hat\beta_1 x_0 + \cdots + \hat\beta_d x_0^d.
              </md>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Use this model to estimate the pointwise variance <m>\operatorname{Var}(\hat f(x_0))</m>.</p></li>
            <li>Draw two standard deviations away from the fitted line to obtain an approximate 95% confidence interval.</li>
          </ul>
          </p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We notice that the data contains two groups: high earners, and low earners. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Most of the data is low earners (about 79 high earners only out of 3000 data points). 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A global model (such as polynomial regression) will impose global structure on the data.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                       <term>Step functions</term> allow learning localized models. 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>

  </paragraphs>



  <paragraphs>
    <title>Step functions</title>

<image source="figures/example_step_functions.png" width="40%">
<shortdescription>A 3 by 1 panel showing identical axes with X on the horizontal axis, and 3 different indicator functions on the vertical axis. The plots show the regions where the indicator function is either zero or one. </shortdescription>
</image>


    <sidebyside widths="44% 43%">
      
        <p>More generally: 
<md>
  <mrow>C_0(X) \amp = I(X \lt c_1),</mrow>
  <mrow>C_1(X) \amp = I(c_1 \le X \lt c_2),</mrow>
  <mrow>C_2(X) \amp = I(c_2 \le X \lt c_3),</mrow>
  <mrow>\amp \vdots</mrow>
  <mrow>C_{K-1}(X) \amp = I(c_{K-1} \le X \lt c_K),</mrow>
  <mrow>C_K(X) \amp = I(c_K \le X),</mrow>
</md>
            
        </p>

        <image source="figures/general_step_functions.png">
        <shortdescription>A four by one panel showing identical axes with X on the horizontal axis, and four different indicator functions on the vertical axis. The plots show the regions where the indicator function is either zero or one for K indicator functions.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      
    </sidebyside>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Choose values for the <term>knots</term> <m>c_1,\ldots,c_K</m>.</p></li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>I(\cdot)</m> is an <term>indicator function</term>. It returns 1 if its argument is true, and 0 otherwise. 
                    </p>
                </li>            
            <li><p>Use indicator functions to break up the range of <m>X</m> into bins, then fit a new constant in each bin.</p></li>
            <li><p>The <m>C_j</m> terms are sometimes also called dummy variables.</p></li>
            <li><p>Note that <m>\sum_j C_j(X)=1</m> because <m>X</m> belongs to exactly one interval.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </p>

            <p>
        <ul>
            <li><p>Step functions allow models to be learned without imposing a global structure.</p></li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                         They are very popular in biostatistics and epidemiology. 5-year age groups are often used to define the bins.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs>
    <title>Step function: Learned model</title>
    <p>
      <md>
        y_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 C_1(x_i) + \beta_2 C_2(x_i) + \cdots + \beta_K C_K(x_i) + \varepsilon_i
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Note that we do not learn a coefficient for <m>C_0</m>. As with qualitative predictors, its value can be determined from the other coefficients.</p></li>
        <li><p>If <m>X \lt c_1</m>, all predictors are zero, so <m>\beta_0</m> is the mean value of <m>Y</m> for <m>X \lt c_1</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>For <m>X\in[c_j,c_{j+1})</m>, the response is <m>\beta_0+\beta_j</m>. Thus, <m>\beta_j</m> is the average change in response for <m>X</m> in that interval relative to <m>X \lt c_1</m>.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

<example>
    <title>Step function example</title>
    <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Given knots <m>c_1=3</m>, <m>c_2=5</m>, and <m>c_3=7</m>, determine the entries in the columns for <m>C_i(X)</m> in the matrix below.            
        </p>
     
        
          <tabular>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <row>
              <cell><m>X</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_0(X)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_1(X)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_2(X)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_3(X)</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row><cell>1</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>2</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>3</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>4</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>5</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>6</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>7</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>8</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>9</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>10</cell><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
            <row><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/><cell/></row>
          </tabular>
          </statement>


<answer>
              <tabular>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <col width="20%"/>
            <row>
              <cell><m>X</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_0(X)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_1(X)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_2(X)</m></cell>
              <cell><m>C_3(X)</m></cell>
            </row>
            <row><cell>1</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            <row><cell>2</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            <row><cell>3</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            <row><cell>5</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            <row><cell>5</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            <row><cell>6</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>0</cell></row>
            <row><cell>7</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
            <row><cell>8</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
            <row><cell>9</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
            <row><cell>10</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell></row>
          </tabular>
</answer>
           </task>
   
    <task>
        <statement>

                <p>My code, using regression on the step function inputs, returned the function    
      <md>
        F(X) = -1 + 3C_1(X) + 4C_2(X) - 2C_3(X).
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>Fill in the missing values in the table below.
            </p>
            <tabular>
            <col width="50%"/>
            <col width="50%"/>
            <row><cell><m>X</m></cell><cell><m>F(X)</m></cell></row>
            <row><cell>1</cell><cell/></row>            
            <row><cell>2</cell><cell/></row>            
            <row><cell>3</cell><cell/></row>            
            <row><cell>4</cell><cell/></row>            
            <row><cell>5</cell><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>6</cell><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>7</cell><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>8</cell><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>9</cell><cell/></row>
            <row><cell>10</cell><cell/></row>            
          </tabular>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <tabular>
            <col width="50%"/>
            <col width="50%"/>
            <row><cell><m>X</m></cell><cell><m>F(X)</m></cell></row>
            <row><cell>1</cell><cell>-1</cell></row>            
            <row><cell>2</cell><cell>-1</cell></row>            
            <row><cell>3</cell><cell>2</cell></row>            
            <row><cell>4</cell><cell>2</cell></row>            
            <row><cell>5</cell><cell>3</cell></row>
            <row><cell>6</cell><cell>3</cell></row>
            <row><cell>7</cell><cell>-3</cell></row>
            <row><cell>8</cell><cell>-3</cell></row>
            <row><cell>9</cell><cell>-3</cell></row>
            <row><cell>10</cell><cell>-3</cell></row>            
          </tabular>
        </answer>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Draw the function.
            </p>
            <image source="figures/StepFunctionAxes.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>Empty Cartesian coordinate grid with x-axis and y-axis.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </statement>
        <hint>
<p>
Using the cutpoints <m>c_1 = 3</m>, <m>c_2 = 5</m>, and <m>c_3 = 7</m>, we can define the functions
  <md>
    <mrow>C_1(X) \amp = I(3 \le X \lt 5)</mrow>
    <mrow>C_2(X) \amp = I(5 \le X \lt 7)</mrow>
    <mrow>C_3(X) \amp = I(7 \le X)</mrow>
  </md>
</p>
        </hint>
        <hint>
            
                <p>
  The piecewise values for <m>F(X)</m> can be written as
  <md>
    F(X) = \begin{cases}
      -1 \amp \text{if } X \lt 3 \\
      2  \amp \text{if } 3 \le X \lt 5 \\
      3  \amp \text{if } 5 \le X \lt 7 \\
      -3 \amp \text{if } X \ge 7
    \end{cases}
  </md>

            </p>
        </hint>
        <answer>
<image source="figures/step_function_example1.png" width="75%">
<shortdescription>A grid showing the solution to the problem which is composed of horizontal lines at the specified cut points.</shortdescription>
</image>
        </answer>
    </task>

    </example>

    
  






  <paragraphs>
    <title>Step function with the <c>Wage</c> data</title>
    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        
          <image source="figures/Fig7-2a.png" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>Scatter plot of Wage versus Age with a green step function fit and dashed standard error bands.</shortdescription>
          </image>        

      
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Good: The fitted model identifies a peak where the high earners are.</p></li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Not great: Unless there are natural breakpoints in the predictors, the model can miss important information. For example, the first bin misses the increasing trend of wage with age. 
                    </p>
                </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
        </sidebyside>
  </paragraphs>

<exercise>
<title>Coffee shop sales step regression</title>
<introduction>
    <p>
        


      Suppose we want to predict the number of coffees sold per hour (<m>Y</m>) at a campus coffee shop based on the time of day (<m>X</m>, measured in hours since opening from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM, where <m>X \in [0,8]</m>). 
    </p>
    <p>
      A sample dataset of 20 hourly observations is provided in <xref ref="table-coffee-sales-data"/>.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="table-coffee-sales-data">
      <title>Hourly Coffee Shop Sales Data</title>
      <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Observation</cell>
          <cell>Time of Day (<m>X</m>)</cell>
          <cell>Coffees Sold (<m>Y</m>)</cell>
        </row>
        <row><cell>1</cell><cell>0.2</cell><cell>48</cell></row>
        <row><cell>2</cell><cell>0.5</cell><cell>54</cell></row>
        <row><cell>3</cell><cell>1.1</cell><cell>51</cell></row>
        <row><cell>4</cell><cell>1.8</cell><cell>45</cell></row>
        <row><cell>5</cell><cell>2.5</cell><cell>52</cell></row>
        <row><cell>6</cell><cell>3.1</cell><cell>118</cell></row>
        <row><cell>7</cell><cell>3.4</cell><cell>125</cell></row>
        <row><cell>8</cell><cell>3.9</cell><cell>115</cell></row>
        <row><cell>9</cell><cell>4.2</cell><cell>122</cell></row>
        <row><cell>10</cell><cell>4.7</cell><cell>120</cell></row>
        <row><cell>11</cell><cell>5.0</cell><cell>42</cell></row>
        <row><cell>12</cell><cell>5.3</cell><cell>38</cell></row>
        <row><cell>13</cell><cell>5.8</cell><cell>40</cell></row>
        <row><cell>14</cell><cell>6.1</cell><cell>82</cell></row>
        <row><cell>15</cell><cell>6.4</cell><cell>78</cell></row>
        <row><cell>16</cell><cell>6.9</cell><cell>85</cell></row>
        <row><cell>17</cell><cell>7.2</cell><cell>77</cell></row>
        <row><cell>18</cell><cell>7.5</cell><cell>81</cell></row>
        <row><cell>19</cell><cell>7.8</cell><cell>84</cell></row>
        <row><cell>20</cell><cell>8.0</cell><cell>79</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>

</introduction>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
        Fit an intercept-only model (0 cutpoints). Is this a good model?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
        If we fit an intercept-only model, <m>F(X) = \beta_0</m>, our best prediction is the global average of all observations:
            <md>\beta_0 \approx 79.55 \text{ coffees/hour}</md>
            This model underfits significantly, completely missing the morning and afternoon fluctuations.
    </p>
  </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Introduce a single split at noon to segment the data into Morning (<m>X \lt 4</m>) and Afternoon (<m>X \ge 4</m>). Fit the resulting model. 
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p> The resulting model with one cutpoint at noon is
             <md>F(X) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 I(X \ge 4).</md>
            Averaging the subsets yields:
            <ul>
              <li><p>Morning Step (Observations 1-8): <m>\beta_0 = 63.5</m></p></li>
              <li><p>Afternoon Step (Observations 9-20): <m>\beta_0 + \beta_1 = 78.83 \implies \beta_1 = 15.33</m></p></li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Can you think of a partition that can better capture the peak hours' rush? There is not a unique solution to this part.
        </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
        <p>
           We can use 3 cutpoints at <m>c_1 = 3.0, c_2 = 5.0, c_3 = 6.0</m>. We fit the model
            <md>F(X) = \beta_0 + \beta_1 C_1(X) + \beta_2 C_2(X) + \beta_3 C_3(X)</md>
            where:
            <md>
              <mrow>C_1(X) \amp = I(3.0 \le X \lt 5.0)</mrow>
              <mrow>C_2(X) \amp = I(5.0 \le X \lt 6.0)</mrow>
              <mrow>C_3(X) \amp = I(6.0 \le X)</mrow>
            </md>
            Calculating the averages within each interval yields:
            <md>
              F(X) = \begin{cases}
                50.0 \amp \text{if } X \lt 3.0  \\
                121.0 \amp \text{if } 3.0 \le X \lt 5.0 \\
                40.0 \amp \text{if } 5.0 \le X \lt 6.0  \\
                80.71 \amp \text{if } X \ge 6.0 
              \end{cases}
            </md>
            This refinement level seems to capture the underlying demand wave of the shop without overfitting. 
        </p>
    </solution>

    </task> 

  
</exercise>

