      Tasks:
      <ol marker="1.">
        <li><p>Calculate the 0-cutpoint global baseline prediction (<m>\beta_0</m>).</p></li>
        <li><p>Fit a 1-cutpoint model splitting the day at exactly noon (<m>c_1 = 12.0</m>).</p></li>
        <li><p>Fit a high-refinement 4-cutpoint step regression model using the cutpoints <m>c_1 = 5.0, c_2 = 9.0, c_3 = 18.0,</m> and <m>c_4 = 21.0</m>.</p></li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Independent Plotting Task: Consider a hypothetical 3-cutpoint model producing the estimated step function:
            <md>G(X) = 15 + 35 C_1(X) + 75 C_2(X) - 5 C_3(X)</md>
            where the indicator basis functions are left-closed and right-open with cutpoints at <m>c_1 = 6.0, c_2 = 10.0,</m> and <m>c_3 = 17.0</m>. Sketch a plot of <m>G(X)</m> from <m>X = 0</m> to <m>X = 24</m>. Explicitly show open vs. closed boundary behaviors at each change point.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      For the independent plotting task, parse <m>G(X)</m> tier-by-tier before sketching. For instance, when <m>X \lt 6.0</m>, all indicators are zero, so <m>G(X) = 15</m>. Remember that left-closed, right-open steps (<m>c_k \le X \lt c_{k+1}</m>) require a solid boundary dot (<m>\bullet</m>) on the left edge and a hollow circular marker (<m>\circ</m>) on the right edge of each horizontal line segment.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Below are the computed solutions for the exercise tasks:
      <ol marker="A.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Global Baseline (0 Cutpoints): The average of all 20 observations is:
            <md>\beta_0 = 41.65 \text{ thousand requests/min}</md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            1 Cutpoint at <m>c_1 = 12.0</m>:
            <ul>
              <li><p>Interval <m>[0, 12)</m> (Obs 1-9): <m>\beta_0 = 30.22</m></p></li>
              <li><p>Interval <m>[12, 24)</m> (Obs 10-20): <m>\beta_0 + \beta_1 = 51.0 \implies \beta_1 = 20.78</m></p></li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            4 Cutpoints (<m>5.0, 9.0, 18.0, 21.0</m>): The piecewise regression function evaluates to the following tier averages:
            <md>
              F(X) = \begin{cases}
                10.0 \amp \text{if } 0 \le X \lt 5.0 \\
                40.0 \amp \text{if } 5.0 \le X \lt 9.0 \\
                100.0 \amp \text{if } 9.0 \le X \lt 18.0 \\
                60.0 \amp \text{if } 18.0 \le X \lt 21.0 \\
                11.5 \amp \text{if } 21.0 \le X \le 24.0
              \end{cases}
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Independent Plotting Task Solution: Evaluating the linear combination for <m>G(X)</m> yields four distinct flat horizontal steps across the day:
            <md>
              G(X) = \begin{cases}
                15 \amp \text{if } 0 \le X \lt 6.0 \\
                50 \amp \text{if } 6.0 \le X \lt 10.0 \pmod{15 + 35} \\
                125 \amp \text{if } 10.0 \le X \lt 17.0 \pmod{15 + 35 + 75} \\
                120 \amp \text{if } 17.0 \le X \le 24.0 \pmod{15 + 35 + 75 - 5}
              \end{cases}
            </md>
            The resulting graph features four horizontal flat lines. Solid dots must be placed at the left boundaries: <m>(0, 15), (6, 50), (10, 125), (17, 120)</m>. Hollow circles must be placed at the right boundaries where transitions occur: <m>(6, 15), (10, 50), (17, 125)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <image source="figures/ecommerce_traffic_plotting_problem.png" width="75%">
    <shortdescription>Step regression for ecommerce traffic plotting problem.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </solution>
</exercise> -->


  </handout>